WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.155
Hi, I'm Elder!

00:00:02.155 --> 00:00:04.751
Jev picks one of the options you send it.

00:00:04.751 --> 00:00:08.213
Here's how it answers, and the two separate reasons that answer moves.

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:13.579
Jev reads its answer off one forward pass, so nothing is sampled.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:17.800
Yet resending a question moves it, and reordering the options moves it more.

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:23.939
You send a question, its evidence, and a menu of options: seventy-seven

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:25.542
of them, in this benchmark.

00:00:25.962 --> 00:00:28.396
Jev scores them in one forward pass.

00:00:28.396 --> 00:00:31.874
No seed, no temperature: nothing is drawn token by token.

00:00:32.294 --> 00:00:36.029
Out comes a probability for every option, rounded to two decimals.

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:39.149
The answer is always one you sent.

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:41.850
That's the no-hallucination claim: closure, not correctness.

00:00:43.483 --> 00:00:45.430
A letter readout picks a token.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:49.974
On one open model, slot A alone was worth about one point seven logits.

00:00:50.394 --> 00:00:52.695
A per-option scorer rates each option alone.

00:00:52.695 --> 00:00:56.968
Shuffle the menu and only the rows shuffle: no order effect at all.

00:00:58.483 --> 00:01:00.696
Not sampling: there's nothing to sample.

00:01:01.116 --> 00:01:04.194
Not the version: every response named the same pinned model.

00:01:04.614 --> 00:01:07.756
Not the client: identical input tokens, every time.

00:01:08.176 --> 00:01:10.797
What's left is serving: batches, kernels, the fleet.

00:01:10.797 --> 00:01:14.400
The reasoned guess: if Jev is a mixture of experts, routing

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.710
depends on the batch.

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:19.658
The published thirteen percent pools both effects.

00:01:19.658 --> 00:01:23.625
Split them: twelve items flip with the order, five with nothing

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:25.068
changed, four with both.

00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:30.122
The answer is always one you offered, but it isn't a property

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:31.394
of the question alone.

00:01:31.394 --> 00:01:33.301
Average a few orderings before thresholding.

00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:38.805
To recap: one pass, two kinds of drift, and one published

00:01:38.805 --> 00:01:40.012
number that held both.

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:42.445
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:42.445 --> 00:01:43.020
I'm Elder.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:43.308
Bye!
